A question I keep revisiting as an SDE working with AI in real-world products. As language models become key tools in our dev workflows and applications, one decision keeps resurfacing:

👉 Should I run models locally or purchase API credits from proprietary providers?

After testing both options, here’s a breakdown from the trenches — including some practical tools and examples that helped me navigate the trade-offs.

⚙️ 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗟𝗟𝗠𝘀 𝗟𝗼𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗹𝘆

🤔Challenges:
 • Model size constraints - Demands decent hardware (especially for >7B param models).
 • Setup can be finicky: tokenizers, quantization, memory tuning, batching, and model loading all require attention.
 • Updates and performance tuning are on you.
 • Faster battery drainage than typical tasks.
 • Initial download - The first time using a model needs to download several GBs, can take a few minutes depending on the connection.

👍Benefits:
 • Total control — no rate limits or surprise outages.
 • Long-term savings for inference-heavy apps.
 • Data stays local — ideal for security-sensitive workflows.
 • Works like a charm in offline mode
 • Lower latency due to no network communication

💡Examples:
 • Use Ollama to run models like llama3, mistral, or gemma locally with just one command.
 • Combine with LangChain or llama.cpp for more customization and chaining logic.

🔧Use Cases:
 • Internal dev tools and code assistants where latency and privacy matter.
 • On-device AI features for desktop or edge apps.
 • Batch processing tasks where you control throughput.
 • Situations where internet connectivity is limited or restricted.

☁️ 𝗨𝘀𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗽𝗿𝗶𝗲𝘁𝗮𝗿𝘆 𝗔𝗣𝗜𝘀

🤔Challenges:
 • Costs can add up fast with high-volume or long-context usage.
 • Limited visibility into how models reason or respond.
 • Data governance and privacy policies may be restrictive in regulated environments.

👍Benefits:
 • Zero setup — go from idea to working prototype in hours.
 • Access to cutting-edge models with continuous improvements.
 • No need to manage infrastructure or deal with GPU provisioning.

💡Examples:
 • OpenAI
 • Anthropic (Claude)
 • Cohere, Mistral API, Groq, Google AI Studio, Together.ai: All offer free or subsidized starter tiers for developers.

🔧Use Cases:
 • MVPs, UX-focused features, or public-facing applications where reliability and model quality matter most.
 • Teams without infra expertise or time for tuning.


✌️Personally I've found running LLMs locally using Ollama to be very fruitful. Just pulling the required model from the registry and spinning it up quickly serves the purpose well. I usually prefer a multimodal model like gemma3:4b.

